// their ids: `hodge-decomposition` from ../../algebraic-geometry/ (area "basic"), `riemann-hilbert-correspondence` from
// ../../representation-theory/ (area "basic").
//
// Model as in ../../topology/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are the standard
// textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a proof that
// rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`). `area` is the three-character MSC class, as in
// data/concepts.js. `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// References to theorems of other folders: "ana:" (Cauchy's integral formula, argument principle, Arzelà–Ascoli,
// Riesz representation, Fredholm alternative, Taylor), "top:" (Stokes, Hodge theorem on harmonic forms, partitions of
// unity, Sard, Baire, Morse theory, Hurewicz), "ca:" (Hilbert basis theorem, Nakayama), "ag:" (GAGA), "geo:"
// (Calabi–Yau theorem; its first MSC class is 53C, so it stays in geometry).
// GAGA and the Kodaira vanishing theorem have 14F as first class and stay in algebraic geometry.
//
// Sources: Hörmander, An Introduction to Complex Analysis in Several Variables (3rd ed. 1990); Gunning and Rossi (1965);
// Grauert and Remmert, Coherent Analytic Sheaves (1984) and Theory of Stein Spaces (1979); Krantz (2nd ed. 1992);
// Griffiths and Harris (1978); Huybrechts, Complex Geometry (2005); Voisin, Hodge Theory I (2002); Kobayashi,
// Hyperbolic Complex Spaces (1998); Milnor, Singular Points of Complex Hypersurfaces (1968). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

